{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And this is the contribution that you shall take from them.</b> According to the view of our Sages, of blessed memory (Sifrei Devarim 1), their contribution was to atone for the gold of the Calf, and according to this view we may explain that the three contributions mentioned here correspond to three transgressions they committed in the act of the Calf. In two of them all the worshippers were equal, and in the third they differed — some less and some more; therefore they brought three contributions, but in two of them they were all equal. First: that they accepted the Calf as a god, saying, ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 32:4). And in this sin all the worshippers were equal, for they all called the Calf a god and a lord; therefore they brought a first contribution in which all were equal, and from it were made all the sockets (אֲדָנִים), for this is derived from the word ״אָדוֹן״ (lord), to atone for their calling the Calf a god. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sanhedrin 63a) that they longed for many gods — that is, for many lords; therefore from it were made many sockets (אֲדָנִים). Second: that they sacrificed to it, as it says, ״וַיִּזְבְּחוּ לוֹ״ (Exodus 32:6). And in this sacrifice too they were all equal; therefore came a second contribution in which all were equal, and from it were made the communal offerings, to atone for ״וַיִּזְבְּחוּ לוֹ״. Third: that which is said, ״וַיִּתְפָּרְקוּ נִזְמֵי הַזָּהָב״ and so on (Exodus 32:3), for they made a donation to the work of the Calf from their property, and the hand of each person was not equal in it, for the rich gave more than the poor. Corresponding to this came a third contribution in which they were not all equal, for each gave according to his wealth. And for this reason the donation of the princes — the shoham stones — is listed last, because for the Calf too the wealthy gave more than the poor, and therefore their donation is mentioned last, by way of penalty, because they had a large share in this sin. And also according to its plain sense, HaShem did not insist on the grandeur of the donation, but on the quality of the givers, for sometimes an honored man may give a little silver from the offering of his hand, while some wicked and violent rich man gives precious stones — and without doubt the donation of the righteous is more accepted; therefore the shoham stones are listed last." "And the fact that for all of them Scripture uses not the language of giving but the language of taking — this is because everyone who gives a donation takes more than he gives, for HaShem adds to him again and again, double and twofold. And this is the view of the Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 33:6), which says: A person buys an object — can the owner be taken along with it? But the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah to Israel and said: ״Take Me along with it,״ as it says, ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה״ — as it were, Me you are taking. And one may raise the question: what does the Torah have to do with the contribution? Rather, just as one who learns Torah with his fellow receives more than he gives, as they said (Ta'anit 7a), ״and from my students, more than all,״ so too with this contribution — he takes more than he gives. And this is what that Midrash concludes: In the way of the world, when one sells an object — then the seller is sad and the buyer is happy; but the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah and rejoiced, as it says (Proverbs 4:2), ״כִּי לֶקַח טוֹב נָתַתִּי לָכֶם, תּוֹרָתִי אַל תַּעֲזֹבוּ״ — 'For I have given you a good lekach (taking); do not forsake My Torah.' And the explanation of this matter is that anyone who sells his fellow some matter of Torah and learns with him — then he too receives from him, and every act of giving has an aspect of taking. And therefore he brings proof from the verse ״כִּי לֶקַח טוֹב,״ and this is a clear, sound, and good proof that an act of giving is called an act of taking. So too with this contribution the act of giving is called an act of taking, and therefore the author of this Midrash based all his words on the verse ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה.״" "<b>Gold and silver and bronze.</b> Even though in the Midrash Tanchuma it is concluded that there were thirteen items, and so Rashi explained, yet when you enumerate them you find fifteen items; and the Ramban (Ra'em) resolved this by noting that תְּכֵלֶת and אַרְגָּמָן and תוֹלַעַת שָׁנִי are all one kind of wool and were counted as one, and the Chizkuni resolved it in another manner. But most of the commentators agreed that there were fifteen items, and this matter accords with what many commentators wrote, that the entire Tabernacle was built on the pattern of three worlds, and so Rabbeinu Bachaye wrote, and the Akeidah, and the Toldot Yitzchak; and Mahar'i(a) wrote that even the sages of the nations agreed upon this design, and it is written (Isaiah 26:4), ״כִּי בְיָהּ ה׳ צוּר עוֹלָמִים״ — that all the worlds were created with the Name of יָ״הּ. Therefore the entire donation for the Tabernacle and its vessels came in the number fifteen, to indicate that the existence of three worlds depends on this Tabernacle, which is built on the pattern of this design. And this is somewhat indicated by those fifteen steps that led from the Women's Courtyard to the Men's Courtyard, and which were built by Shlomo, who was in the fifteenth generation from Avraham. And the commentators wrote further that man himself, who is called a small world, is also built on the pattern of this design, and it appears that this is why it says, ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם״ (Exodus 25:8). It should have said ״בְּתוֹכוֹ״ (in it) — but rather, it means to say that through the agency of this design in which the glory of HaShem dwells, He will also dwell within them — within Israel, in their very bodies. For the essence of His Presence is there for the sake of Israel, not for the sake of this design made of silver and gold; for it is nothing but a means to draw, through its agency, the glory of HaShem into the midst of the children of Israel." "And in the Midrash Tanchuma it is concluded: gold corresponds to the kingdom of Babylonia, as it says (Daniel 2:38), ״אַנְתְּ רֵאשָׁה דִּי דַהֲבָא״ — 'You are the head of gold'; and silver corresponds to Media, as it says of it, ״וַעֲשֶׂרֶת אֲלָפִים כִּכַּר כֶּסֶף״ and so on (Esther 3:9); and bronze corresponds to the kingdom of Greece, which is the least of them all; ״עוֹרֹת אֵילִים מְאָדָּמִים״ corresponds to the kingdom of Edom, and so on. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'Even though you see four kingdoms exulting and coming upon you, by your life I will cause salvation to sprout for you from within the subjugation.' What is written after it? ״שֶׁמֶן לַמָּאוֹר״ (Exodus 25:6) — this is King Mashiach, as it says (Psalms 132:17), ״שָׁם אַצְמִיחַ קֶרֶן לְדָוִד, עָרַכְתִּי נֵר לִמְשִׁיחִי,״ and it is written (Isaiah 60:1), ״קוּמִי אוֹרִי כִּי בָא אוֹרֵךְ.״ And many have wondered at this Midrash and its like, that they remove the verse from its plain sense to interpret it regarding the four kingdoms." "And it appears to me that even the author of this Midrash holds that everything is according to its plain sense, and they do not allude at all to the four kingdoms; rather, his intention is to explain why the Holy One, blessed be He, showed the wicked Nevuchadnetzar all four kingdoms — and why they were likened to gold and silver and bronze and iron. And was the wicked man worthy of this, that HaShem, blessed be He, should inform him of what would come after him? Rather, surely His intention was for the benefit of Israel, so that they would know that they have in their hand merits through which they are able to stand against all these four kingdoms, and that they [the kingdoms] would not be able to destroy Israel. For the kingdom of Babylonia was likened to gold, and Israel has opposite it one commandment performed with gold — and that is the gold of the wave-offering, and this gold stands opposite the kingdom of Babylonia. The expression ״כְּנֶגֶד״ (opposite/against) indicates that this gold opposes that kingdom which was likened to gold. And likewise silver is opposite Media, and likewise bronze is opposite Greece, and likewise עוֹרֹת אֵילִים מְאָדָּמִים (reddened ram skins) are opposite Edom, which was likened to iron — for from it [from such skins/leather] they make the reddening sword. And a general principle for all of them: what we find in the tractate (Megillah 13a), Rabbi Levi said: 'It was revealed and known before the One who spoke and the world came into being that Haman was destined to weigh shekels against Israel; therefore He preceded their shekels to Haman's shekels.' And from here there is proof that this silver came to precede Haman — that is, the kingdom of Media. So too this gold came to precede Nevuchadnetzar, and this bronze came to precede Greece, and עוֹרֹת אֵילִים מְאָדָּמִים to precede Edom. And through this the expression ״כְּנֶגֶד,״ stated regarding all of them, is precisely accounted for, for these commandments are opposed (מִתְנַגְּדִים) to them. But regarding ״שֶׁמֶן לַמָּאוֹר״ it did not say that it is 'opposite' (כְּנֶגֶד) King Mashiach, for there is no opposition here, but rather one matter — that in the merit of ״שֶׁמֶן לַמָּאוֹר״ HaShem will prepare a lamp for His anointed. And so I saw in the Tanchuma that it used the expression ״כְּנֶגֶד״ regarding all of them, unlike the reading of the Yalkut, in which the expression ״כְּנֶגֶד״ is not written in some of them. And so the view of the author of this Midrash is to indicate through which merit Israel has endurance against these four kingdoms, and this is what he concludes: 'Even though you see …' and so on."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The text reads מַהֲרִי״א which may refer to Mahar'i (Rabbi Yosef Ibn Kaspi) or Mahari (Rabbi Yitzchak); the additional aleph in the acronym 